we've had a statement from the duke of  cambridge prince william  paying tribute to his grandfather the  duke of edinburgh in the statement  he said my grandfather's century of life  was defined by service to his country  and commonwealth to his wife and queen  and to our family  i feel lucky to have had not just had  his example to guide me  but his enduring presence well into my  own adult life both  through good times and the hardest days  i will always be grateful  that my wife had so many years to get to  know my grandfather  and for the kindness he showed her i  will never take for granted  the special memories my children will  always have of their great grandpa  coming to collect them in his carriage  and seeing for themselves his  infectious sense of adventure as well as  his mischievous  sense of humour my grandfather was an  extraordinary man  and part of an extraordinary generation  catherine and i will continue to do what  he would have wanted  and will support the queen in the years  ahead i miss my grandpa  but i know he would want us to get on  with the job  let's go to our royal correspondent  rhiannon mills who's in windsor for us  now  a deeply personal uh statement there  from  the duke of cambridge following the  death of his grandpa  it is and i think we anticipated that we  were likely to hear from the duke of  edinburgh's grandchildren  at some point today after all over the  weekend we heard from  his four children paying their own  tributes talking about that significant  loss  at the void in the queen's life that  there now is following the death of  prince philip  and now yes we are starting to hear the  tributes from his grandchildren  accompanying that statement there was  also released a photograph  showing at the duke of edinburgh and  sitting  on a carriage because we know he was  keen very keen on his carriage driving  he actually  drew up the international rule book for  carriage driving and sitting alongside  him  is a little prince george and so a very  touching photograph that prince william  and kate have decided to release  alongside that statement  but i think if we if we look at some of  the things that he had to say  reflecting on the fact that his  grandfather's century of life was  defined as he says  by service to the country the  commonwealth  to the queen and also our family talk  about how he feels lucky that he had his  example to guide him that his enduring  presence  well into his adult life both through  good times  and the hardest days and of course that  does make us think back to the fact that  it was  prince philip who encouraged prince  william to walk behind  princess diana's coffin during those  very difficult days  and back in 1997 when the country  mourned  the death of the princess of wales and  he also says i will never take for  granted the special memories that my  childhood will always have  my children sorry will always have of  their great grandpa coming to collect  them in his carriage and of course we  have seen  that photograph that he's been and  that's been released  but also i think it's it's really  wonderful to hear prince william there  talking  about the duke's infectious sense of  adventure  as well as his mischievous sense of  humor and i think what really stands out  to me is the final sentence  in that statement where he says i know  he would want us to get on  with the job and i think that's what  we're going to see from the royal family  this week  and certainly in the weeks ahead  following the funeral  on saturday i anticipate that yes they  will go into that they are now in this  two-week period of mourning but we have  been told that there will still be some  engagements where  it is deemed to be appropriate and also  for the royal family as well very  important to come out and pay their  thanks to all the members of public  who've been  paying their tributes and i think share  this moment  with the country and people around the  world
 

